  "worked_solution": "To map the entire region $\u0394$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u-v,\\,u(u-{v}^{3})) \\\\  & =(u-0,\\,u(u-{0}^{3})) \\\\  & =(u,\\,{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y={u}^{2},\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)={t}^{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=y\\,\u21d2\\,y={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a upward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{1}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,0)\\to (0-0,\\,0(0-{0}^{3}))=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(1,0)\\to (1-0,\\,1(1-{0}^{3}))=(1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{1}$ is an arc of a upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/7aa56f8bba41a352a02c6d26554c417e7844cd60c28078da51cbf16a3220e57d.png)",
